---
interface Props {
	title: string;
	description: string;
}

const { title, description } = Astro.props;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<meta charset="utf-8" />
		<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
		<meta name="generator" content={Astro.generator} />
		<title>{title}</title>
		<meta name="description" content={description} />
		<meta name="color-scheme" content="dark" />
		{/*
			Mirrors --color-bg in src/styles/global.css. A meta tag cannot read a CSS custom property,
			and without it a phone browser paints its own light chrome above a dark page.
		*/}
		<meta name="theme-color" content="#0d1311" />
		{/*
			The whole privacy claim of this app is "your token only ever goes to
			classic.warcraftlogs.com". A connect-src policy is the only version of that claim a visitor
			can verify without reading our source, so ship one — the browser, not our code, enforces it.
			It names the single MoP host rather than a wildcard, because that is the only host this app
			has any business reaching. Production-only, because `astro dev` talks to an HMR websocket
			this would block.

			The rest of the policy exists so that `connect-src` is not the only line in it. Read top to
			bottom:

			  default-src 'none'  nothing that is not named below may load at all. That covers workers,
			                      frames, media and manifests, none of which this app has.
			  script-src          'self' is the bundled islands; wow.zamimg.com is the tooltip widget.
			  style-src           the same two, plus inline — see below, it is not a choice.
			  img-src             every icon is Wowhead's; data: is the inline SVG spinner.
			  font-src            there are no webfonts. 'self' keeps a future one from being a third
			                      party's, rather than pretending the slot does not exist.
			  object-src          nothing to embed. Redundant under default-src and kept anyway, because
			                      it is the directive people look for when they audit one of these.
			  base-uri            a stray base element would silently repoint every relative URL here.
			  form-action         there is no form here that should ever submit anywhere.

			What `script-src` buys, stated exactly, because a policy that overstates itself is worse than
			none: it bounds the *hosts* a script may be fetched from, and it withholds 'unsafe-eval'. It
			does not stop injected inline script, because 'unsafe-inline' is present and cannot be
			removed from here. Astro emits three inline bootstrap scripts — the island custom element,
			its loader, and the widget module below, which Astro inlines because it is small — and their
			content is build output, so hashes written by hand would be wrong after the next Astro
			upgrade, in the loudest possible way. `security.csp` in astro.config.mjs generates those
			hashes correctly, but it also hashes styles, and that is fatal here: ApexCharts ships its
			stylesheet as a string and injects a style element per chart at runtime, and a hash in a
			directive makes the browser ignore 'unsafe-inline' in that same directive. Enabling it was
			measured, not guessed: six of the seven style elements on the page stop applying, which is
			all of ApexCharts.

			frame-ancestors is missing on purpose: a meta-tag policy ignores it. It is in public/_headers,
			which Cloudflare Pages serves as a real header, and which is the only place it can work.
		*/}
		{
			import.meta.env.PROD && (
				<meta
					http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy"
					content="default-src 'none'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://wow.zamimg.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://wow.zamimg.com; img-src 'self' data: https://wow.zamimg.com; connect-src 'self' https://classic.warcraftlogs.com; font-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'"
				/>
			)
		}
	</head>
	<body>
		{/*
			Wowhead's tooltip widget, which is the one third-party script this page loads.
			
			It exists because the gear section shows items by icon, and an icon with no name is a
			picture of a puzzle. The log gives an item id and nothing else — no name, no stats — so the
			only way to say what a piece *is* comes from Wowhead, and their widget is how they publish
			it. It attaches to any link pointing at one of their item or spell pages.

			What this costs, stated plainly because the README makes a promise about it: hovering an item
			tells wowhead.com that someone looked at that item. It is loaded from the same image host the
			icons already come from, and it is deferred so it never blocks the report.

			What it can reach is everything this page can reach, and the previous version of this comment
			was wrong about that in the one way that matters. It claimed the widget sees no token because
			the token is in sessionStorage and never enters the DOM. Not being in the DOM is not a
			barrier: `power.js` is executed by us, in our origin, as first-party script, so
			`sessionStorage.getItem('wcl.token')` returns the token to it exactly as it does to our own
			code. Reading the token was never a DOM operation, so keeping it out of the DOM protects it
			from nothing that runs here.

			The policy above does not narrow that either — it *names* wow.zamimg.com in script-src, which
			is the opposite of blocking it — and because Wowhead's own asset host is also in img-src and
			style-src, a URL with the token in its query string is a way out of here as well. A CSP
			cannot un-trust a script it has been told to trust. Loading this widget is a decision the
			page makes about Wowhead, and the honest form of it is: we do not hand them the token or the
			report code, and we believe they do not go looking; nothing enforces the second half.

			Written as a processed script element so it is TypeScript and bundled, but note that
			Astro inlines it into the HTML because it is small — so this is one of the inline scripts
			that 'unsafe-inline' above is paying for, not an external file.

			Tag names are spelled out rather than angle-bracketed throughout these comments on purpose:
			oxlint slices .astro files by scanning for a script open tag, and a literal one inside a
			comment sends it off parsing JavaScript from the middle of a sentence.
		*/}
		<script>
			// The widget reads this before it initialises. Left plain: recolouring and renaming the
			// report's own links to match Wowhead's house style would fight the design.
			Object.assign(window, { whTooltips: { colorLinks: false, iconizeLinks: false, renameLinks: false } });

			const widget = document.createElement('script');
			widget.src = 'https://wow.zamimg.com/widgets/power.js';
			widget.defer = true;
			document.head.append(widget);
		</script>
		<slot />
	</body>
</html>
